I’ve been reading about Linear Congruential Generators (LCGs) and their applications in generating sequences. This got me thinking; what if I wanted to create a C program using an LCG to generate a specific sequence, like "helloworld"? To keep it simple, let's use the mapping where 'a' = 0, 'b' = 1, ..., 'z' = 25. I want to construct a C program that uses an LCG to generate this sequence. Say the sequence is "helloworld".

Here’s what I have in mind:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Function to generate the next number in the sequence using LCG
int lcg(int x, int a, int c, int m) {
    return (a * x + c) % m;
}

int main() {
    // Constants for the LCG
    int a = 1103515245; // Multiplier
    int c = 12345;      // Increment
    int m = 256;        // Modulus
    int x = 0;          // Initial seed

    // Mapping of letters to numbers
    char sequence[] = "helloworld";
    int length = sizeof(sequence) - 1;

    // Generate and print the sequence
    for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        x = lcg(x, a, c, m);
        int letter = x % 26; // Map the generated number to a letter
        printf("%c", 'a' + letter);
    }

    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

I’m curious if anyone has any tips or suggestions on how to improve this or if there are any pitfalls I should be aware of. Thanks!
